# macro_expansion_with_include.tcyr — a function-like `#define` beside an `include`
# (6.6.6 bites 15b + 19f).
#
# WHY THIS TEST EXISTS AND WHY IT IS HERE. Bite 15b fixed two silent macro-expansion defects —
# a macro name matched in the MIDDLE of an identifier, and a macro invocation expanded inside a
# STRING LITERAL — but it could ship NO `.tcyr` for them, because a `.tcyr` must
# `include "lib/assert.cyr"` and at 6.6.5 a function-like `#define` beside ANY include broke
# the build outright. Bite 19f fixed that (PP_SYNC_SRCB: PP_MACRO_PASS read the PREVIOUS pass's
# INPUT instead of its output, so having one function-like macro in scope undid `#ifdef`
# filtering and truncated the source at 1 MB), which is what makes this test writable.
#
# It runs on the cross-host set because the preprocessor is shared but the `#ifdef` arms it
# filters are PER TARGET: on aarch64 / Mach-O / PE the arms that survive are different ones,
# and `lib/assert.cyr`'s own transitive includes carry them.
#
# ⚠ NO `fn` DEFINITIONS IN THIS FILE, ON PURPOSE. The identifier-boundary rows are written with
# globals rather than functions because a `fn`'s braces can REBALANCE a mangled preprocessor
# stream: measured against the 6.6.5 compiler, the same rows written as three `fn`s compiled
# CLEAN and produced a binary that ran nothing — silent, exit 0, indistinguishable from success
# to a runner that reads only the exit code.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THE 6.6.5 COMPILER DOES WITH THIS FILE, MEASURED, because "RED on the old compiler"
# deserves a number rather than a claim. With its prose comments stripped, 6.6.5 exits 1 with
# `error: lib/assert.cyr:120:36: undefined variable 'na'` and emits NO binary. With them
# present it emits a binary that runs NOTHING: silent, exit 0. 6.6.5 ALSO mis-expands inside a
# `#` comment (the defect bite 15d fixed), so the two mangling paths interact and the outcome
# is content-length dependent — removing any ONE comment line does not flip it. Neither
# outcome is a correct run, but only the first is RED to a runner that reads the exit code,
# which is why the mutation-proven detector for 19f is the shell gate
# tests/gates/frontend/macro_pass_preserves_ifdef_filtering.sh and this file is the cross-host
# behavioural pin. CHANGELOG [6.6.6]
#define MEI_ID(a) (a)
#define MEI_WRAP(a) ((a) * 2)
include "lib/assert.cyr"

# Globals whose NAMES contain the macro name. Pre-15b `MEI_ID` matched in the MIDDLE of an
# identifier, so `myMEI_ID` became `my((a))` — a program with both `myMEI_ID` and `my` bound
# the wrong one, and the only sign was a duplicate warning naming a symbol nobody wrote twice.
var myMEI_ID = 101;
var my = 2;
# ...and one whose name STARTS with the macro name: the right-hand boundary. PP_HASH_ID hashes
# to the `(`, so this was never at risk — pinned anyway, so a future "fix" cannot break it.
var MEI_IDX = 1001;

var r1 = assert_eq(MEI_ID(5), 5, "a function-like macro expands beside an include");
var r2 = assert_eq(MEI_WRAP(21), 42, "a macro body with its own parens expands correctly");

var r3 = assert_eq(myMEI_ID, 101, "a macro name inside an identifier is NOT an invocation");
var r4 = assert_eq(my, 2, "...and the shorter global of the same tail is still itself");
var r5 = assert_eq(MEI_IDX, 1001, "a macro name that is a PREFIX of an identifier is not an invocation");

# A string literal holding what looks like an invocation. Pre-15b the string's BYTES were
# rewritten in place, so its length changed: the literal below measured 11 bytes, not 17.
# ⚠ The invocation is deliberately NOT spelled out in this comment: bite 15d found that a
# macro invocation opened in a COMMENT expanded too, so writing it here would exercise a
# different defect and mask this row on an older compiler.
var mei_s = "MEI_ID(5) literal";
var r6 = assert_eq(strlen(mei_s), 17, "a macro invocation inside a string literal is left alone");

# The include itself still works. `assert_summary` below is proof, but pin one of its
# transitive symbols explicitly so a stripped include cannot pass by doing nothing.
var r7 = assert_eq(strlen("abcd"), 4, "the included stdlib (strlen, via assert.cyr) is intact");

# ⚠ THE EXIT PATH IS PART OF THE TEST. The summary lands in a global that is SEEDED non-zero
# and only overwritten by a statement, so a build that emits a binary but drops SOME of the
# top-level program exits 92 rather than a silent, success-shaped 0.
#
# ⚠ AND THE SEED IS NOT ENOUGH WHEN THE WHOLE PROGRAM IS DROPPED — the seed is part of the text
# that vanishes. Measured on a968ed07's cycc, the version of this file that first shipped
# compiled rc 0 to a 43,512-byte binary that printed nothing and exited 0, which a runner
# reading only the exit code scores as a PASS.
#
# ⚠ AND DO NOT READ THE CURRENT rc ON AN OLD COMPILER AS A GUARANTEE — MEASURED WHILE WRITING
# THIS NOTE. Editing this comment block flipped a968ed07's outcome THREE times in a row —
# rc 1 with an empty binary, then rc 0 with a 43,544-byte binary that runs nothing, then rc 1
# again — with no change whatever to the code below. The 6.6.5 mangling is CONTENT-LENGTH
# dependent, exactly as the paragraph above says, so which side of it this file lands on is an
# accident of its byte count and changes when any line is edited, including a comment. Whatever
# an old compiler does with this file today, do not build a claim on it. That is the whole
# reason the defence lives in the runner and not in this file.
# That hole is closed OUTSIDE the binary: scripts/cross-os-libtest-runner.sh now requires the
# "N passed" line that assert_summary prints, for every test whose source calls it, so an empty
# run cannot score a pass on ecb / ach / cass / pi. Pinned by
# tests/gates/toolchain/crossos_runner_rejects_a_silent_binary.sh. CHANGELOG [6.6.6]
var mei_rc = 92;
mei_rc = assert_summary();
syscall(60, mei_rc);
